\begin{abstract}
\noindent Markerless infrared stereo hand trackers, such as the Ultraleap Leap Motion Controller (LMC) and its second-generation successor (LMC2), enable high-frame-rate 3D skeletal interaction for multimedia systems, virtual reality, and 3D character animation. However, hand tracking across optical, depth, and multi-view sensors suffers from two persistent physical limitations: (i) part-level finger self-occlusion and whole-hand temporal burst dropouts caused by line-of-sight obstruction, and (ii) anatomical bone-ratio and camera-distance variations that induce scale-based classification shortcuts and unnatural skeletal distortions during 3D motion retargeting. Existing hand-tracking restoration methods often treat missing skeletal observations as generic temporal imputation, without jointly addressing reference-bone instability, anatomical consistency, and causal streaming constraints. This paper presents a unified restoration-to-multimedia framework centered on three core contributions: (1) a \textbf{Visibility-Guarded Wrist-Centered $L_2$ Palm-Bone Normalization} formulated in both offline non-causal (System A) and zero-lookahead causal sliding-window (System B) variants to prevent division-by-zero explosions and scale shortcuts; (2) a \textbf{Kinematic-Aware Spatio-Temporal Graph Convolutional Network (KAR-STGCN)} integrating a two-stage dilated temporal bridge ($25$-frame receptive field), anatomical graph message passing, bone-length consistency regularization ($\mathcal{L}_{\text{bone}}$), and dual-stream velocity encoding; and (3) \textbf{KAR-Bench Studio}, an open-source diagnostic and Biovision Hierarchy (\texttt{.bvh}) 3D animation retargeting suite. Evaluated under subject-independent cross-validation protocols across four complementary 3D hand benchmarks---distinguishing true pose restoration accuracy on depth-based \textbf{SHREC'17} ($N=28$) and sub-millimeter 6-DoF magnetic \textbf{FPHA} ($N=6$) from real optical sensor transfer on \textbf{LMDHG} ($N=21$) and secondary-sensor cross-view consistency on \textbf{Dual-LMC2 (ML2HP)} ($N=21$, 17 classes)---KAR-STGCN reduces occluded-joint restoration error ($\text{MPJPE}_{\text{miss}}$) relative to CTR-GCN by \textbf{50.6\%} on SHREC'17 Level 3 ($9.42 \pm 0.44\text{ mm}$ vs.\ $19.08 \pm 0.89\text{ mm}$; $\text{MPJPE}_{\text{all}} = 4.18 \pm 0.21\text{ mm}$), \textbf{50.5\%} on SHREC'17 Level 4 ($11.68 \pm 0.54\text{ mm}$ vs.\ $23.58 \pm 1.12\text{ mm}$; $\text{MPJPE}_{\text{all}} = 5.42 \pm 0.27\text{ mm}$), \textbf{45.8\%} on FPHA Level 4 ($15.84 \pm 0.68\text{ mm}$ vs.\ $29.24 \pm 1.38\text{ mm}$; $\text{MPJPE}_{\text{all}} = 8.92 \pm 0.41\text{ mm}$), \textbf{44.0\%} on LMDHG held-out masking ($9.86 \pm 0.45\text{ mm}$ vs.\ $17.62 \pm 0.82\text{ mm}$), and \textbf{45.3\%} on natural Dual-LMC2 cross-view reference dropouts ($9.18 \pm 0.42\text{ mm}$ vs.\ $16.78 \pm 0.79\text{ mm}$, with zero-shot LMC1-to-LMC2 transfer already achieving $12.15 \pm 0.56\text{ mm}$ and $86.40\%$ F1 without target fine-tuning). Furthermore, KAR-STGCN reduces bone-length consistency error (BLCE) to $2.04\text{--}3.85\text{ mm}$ and biomechanical joint-angle violation rates (JAVR) to $1.08\%$ on Dual-LMC2 and $1.15\%$ on SHREC'17 Level 4 (with mean Joint Angle Error $\text{JAE} = 4.12^\circ$), achieving \textbf{$0.93\text{ ms}$} algorithmic streaming latency ($0.48\text{ ms}$ GPU forward pass, $0\text{ frames}$ lookahead, and $1.42\text{ ms}$ application-level \texttt{.bvh} retargeting latency). Subject-level hypothesis tests confirm statistically significant improvements across each individual benchmark ($p_{\text{Holm}} \le 0.0014$, statistical power $>99\%$).

\vspace{3pt}
\noindent \textbf{Keywords:} 3D Hand Pose Restoration $\cdot$ Optical Motion Capture $\cdot$ Leap Motion Controller 2 $\cdot$ Spatio-Temporal Graph Convolutional Network $\cdot$ $L_2$ Bone Normalization $\cdot$ 3D Character Animation Retargeting
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Three-dimensional (3D) skeletal motion capture (MoCap) and hand tracking serve as a foundational interface for interactive multimedia, virtual reality (VR), sign language recognition, and 3D character animation \cite{bregler2007motion,tirakoat2011optimized,noiumkar2013use,marin2016hand,rastgoo2025deep}. While multi-camera passive optical MoCap studios provide sub-millimeter spatial accuracy, they require reflective markers, controlled studio lighting, and labor-intensive post-processing to repair occluded marker trajectories \cite{tirakoat2011optimized,noiumkar2013use}. Consequently, compact markerless infrared (IR) stereo sensors---such as the first-generation Ultraleap Leap Motion Controller (LMC) \cite{marin2016hand,boulahia2017dynamic} and the second-generation Leap Motion Controller 2 (LMC2) \cite{gilmartin2024dual,matuszczyk2025comparative}---alongside depth-based skeletal estimators \cite{desmedt2017shrec,kumar2018position}, have become primary sensing modalities for desktop and head-mounted multimedia systems.

Despite advances in sensor resolution, recent metrological evaluations of the LMC2 \cite{matuszczyk2025comparative}, dual-sensor LMC2 recordings \cite{gilmartin2024dual}, and optical hand tracking studies \cite{boulahia2017dynamic,kumar2018position,liu2024novel} reveal three fundamental technical bottlenecks across hand tracking pipelines:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Part-Level Self-Occlusion vs.\ Whole-Hand Burst Dropouts:} Hand trackers exhibit modality-specific failure modes. Depth-based pose estimators \cite{desmedt2017shrec,kumar2018position} frequently suffer from part-level fingertip self-occlusion and localized coordinate drift when fingers cross the palm. Conversely, metrological evaluations of the LMC2 by Matuszczyk et al.\ \cite{matuszczyk2025comparative} demonstrate palm positional errors of $5.2\text{--}5.3\text{ mm}$ and static repeatability of $0.4\text{--}0.8\text{ mm}$ within the central interaction zone, but show that the tracking SDK exhibits binary whole-hand dropouts (where all 21 joints drop to zero or freeze simultaneously) when the hand reaches oblique viewing angles or workspace boundaries \cite{boulahia2017dynamic,matuszczyk2025comparative}.
    \item \textbf{Scale Shortcuts and 3D Character Retargeting Distortions:} Deep classifiers trained on unnormalized 3D coordinates often exploit camera-to-hand distance or subject hand size as a spurious shortcut rather than learning intrinsic finger kinematics. Moreover, mapping uncalibrated or naively imputed trajectories onto 3D character rigs violates rigid bone-length invariants, causing unnatural finger stretching and hyperextended joint angles \cite{noiumkar2013use}.
    \item \textbf{Normalization Singularity under Reference-Joint Loss:} Standard scale normalization divides coordinates by an instantaneous reference bone length (e.g., wrist-to-middle-MCP distance $\|\mathbf{p}_{t,9}-\mathbf{p}_{t,0}\|_2$). When reference joints drop out or collapse while other joints retain residual coordinates, naive division by a small constant $\epsilon$ triggers numerical explosion ($\sim 10^7\text{--}10^8\text{ mm}$), destabilizing graph message passing.
\end{enumerate}

Existing hand-tracking restoration methods often treat missing skeletal observations as generic temporal imputation, without jointly addressing reference-bone instability, anatomical consistency, and causal streaming constraints. To resolve this research gap without conflating offline post-processing and real-time streaming regimes, we organize our scientific contributions into three core pillars:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Core Contribution 1 (Visibility-Guarded Anatomical Normalization):} We align four complementary 3D hand benchmarks---optical/depth skeletal datasets (SHREC'17 \cite{desmedt2017shrec}, $N=28$; LMDHG \cite{boulahia2017dynamic}, $N=21$), a cross-view optical hardware dataset (Dual-LMC2 ML2HP \cite{gilmartin2024dual}, $N=21$), and a cross-modal 6-DoF magnetic dataset with synthetic missingness (FPHA \cite{garcia2018first}, $N=6$)---into a canonical 21-joint topology and formulate a visibility-guarded wrist-centered $L_2$ palm-bone normalization in two operational regimes: \textbf{System A (Offline Sequence-Median)} and \textbf{System B (Causal Sliding-Window Running-Median)}. We validate its robustness against reference-joint dropouts, wrist/MCP coordinate drift, $5\%\text{--}20\%$ visibility mask corruption, and $\times 0.70\text{--}\times 1.40$ scale perturbations.
    \item \textbf{Core Contribution 2 (Kinematic-Aware Spatio-Temporal Restoration --- KAR-STGCN):} We design a lightweight network ($0.42\text{M}$ parameters, $0.18\text{ GFLOPs}$) that couples a two-stage dilated temporal bridge ($25$-frame receptive field) with anatomical graph convolutions, explicit bone-length consistency regularization ($\mathcal{L}_{\text{bone}}$), and dual-stream position-velocity classification.
    \item \textbf{Core Contribution 3 (Unified Restoration-to-Multimedia Application Framework):} We develop \textbf{KAR-Bench Studio}, an open-source multimedia software suite providing real-time bone-length oscilloscopy, three-tier latency profiling ($0.48\text{ ms}$ GPU model forward pass, $0.93\text{ ms}$ algorithmic streaming latency with $0$-frame lookahead, and $1.42\text{ ms}$ application-level \texttt{.bvh} retargeting latency), TorchScript export, and automated Biovision Hierarchy (\texttt{.bvh}) 3D character retargeting that reduces BLCE to $2.04\text{--}3.85\text{ mm}$ and JAVR to $1.08\%$ on Dual-LMC2 and $1.15\%$ on SHREC'17 Level 4.
\end{itemize}

\section{Related Work}

\subsection{3D Hand Pose Estimation and Optical Tracking Metrology}
Early optical MoCap research established that marker placement optimization and post-capture trajectory cleaning are essential for biomechanical fidelity in sports science and character animation \cite{bregler2007motion,tirakoat2011optimized,noiumkar2013use}. For markerless hand tracking, the first-generation LMC \cite{marin2016hand,boulahia2017dynamic} and second-generation LMC2 \cite{gilmartin2024dual,matuszczyk2025comparative} estimate 3D skeletal joints from stereo IR cameras. Matuszczyk et al.\ \cite{matuszczyk2025comparative} recently quantified LMC2 accuracy ($5.2\text{--}5.3\text{ mm}$ palm error, $0.4\text{--}0.8\text{ mm}$ repeatability) and documented that tracking loss occurs as whole-hand dropouts at extreme angles. While Gil-Mart\'{i}n et al.\ \cite{gilmartin2024dual} introduced the Multi-view Leap Motion Controller 2 Hand Pose (ML2HP) dataset using dual orthogonal LMC2 sensors across 21 subjects and 17 hand postures to mitigate single-view occlusion hardware-wise, single-sensor multimedia deployments require algorithmic restoration. Furthermore, dual-sensor recordings provide an independent secondary-sensor cross-view reference (contextualized by the $\approx 5.2\text{--}5.3\text{ mm}$ central-zone error of LMC2 sensors \cite{matuszczyk2025comparative}) for evaluating single-sensor dropout recovery.

\subsection{Occlusion-Robust Hand Tracking and Time-Series Imputation}
In commercial VR/AR systems, speed-adaptive low-pass filters such as the One-Euro ($1\text{\euro}$) Filter \cite{casiez20121euro} and Rauch-Tung-Striebel (RTS) Kalman smoothers are widely used to suppress high-frequency jitter, but they rely on zero-order hold or linear extrapolation during multi-frame tracking loss. In deep multivariate time-series imputation, bidirectional recurrent models (BRITS \cite{cao2018brits}) and diagonally masked self-attention networks (SAITS \cite{du2023saits}) achieve strong point-wise imputation. However, generic imputers ignore 3D skeletal kinematic chains, causing severe bone-length shrinkage or elongation during rapid finger articulation. Similarly, while single-frame occlusion-robust hand estimators and spatial-temporal graph U-Net motion infillers exploit skeletal hierarchies, they neither guard against reference-bone normalization singularities under streaming wrist/MCP dropouts nor enforce zero-lookahead causal bounds.

\subsection{Skeleton-Based Action and Gesture Recognition}
In \textit{Multimedia Tools and Applications} and computer vision venues, 3D hand gesture recognition has evolved from geometric features \cite{marin2016hand,boulahia2017dynamic,kumar2018position} and fast kinematic 1D-CNNs such as DD-Net \cite{yang2019make} to Spatio-Temporal Graph Convolutional Networks (ST-GCN \cite{yan2018spatial}, 2s-AGCN \cite{shi2019two}, MS-G3D \cite{liu2020disentangling}, CTR-GCN \cite{chen2021channel}) and recent topology-aware architectures such as BlockGCN \cite{zhou2024blockgcn} and residual attention networks \cite{asteasuain2022applying,liu2024novel,rastgoo2025deep,dhanjal2025multilingual}. Because standard GCNs assume fully observed node coordinates, feeding zero-dropped frames directly into graph adjacency layers propagates corrupted features to neighboring joints.

\subsection{Motion Capture Reconstruction and Anatomical Constraints}
Mapping raw or naively smoothed skeletal streams onto 3D character rigs frequently produces visually jarring limb stretching and invalid joint rotations \cite{tirakoat2011optimized,noiumkar2013use}. Unlike disjoint two-stage pipelines that chain a generic time-series imputer with a separate classifier, KAR-STGCN jointly optimizes coordinate imputation, bone-length preservation, and velocity-informed gesture recognition within a single end-to-end architecture.

\section{Proposed Methodology}

\subsection{Canonical 21-Joint Topology Alignment}
We unify four heterogeneous 3D hand datasets into a canonical 21-joint hand graph $\mathcal{G} = (\mathcal{V}, \mathcal{E})$ with $|\mathcal{V}|=21$ joints and $|\mathcal{E}|=20$ anatomical bone segments: wrist ($0$), thumb ($1\text{--}4$), index ($5\text{--}8$), middle ($9\text{--}12$), ring ($13\text{--}16$), and pinky ($17\text{--}20$). For SHREC'17 \cite{desmedt2017shrec} ($J=22$), the redundant palm center (index 1) is removed. For FPHA \cite{garcia2018first} ($J=21$ magnetic sensors), the finger-root-first indexing is bijectively permuted to $\mathcal{V}$. For LMDHG \cite{boulahia2017dynamic} and Dual-LMC2 (ML2HP) \cite{gilmartin2024dual}, we extract the active dominant hand and prune auxiliary forearm/palm-center nodes to retain the exact 21 hand joints matching $\mathcal{V}$.

\textbf{Temporal Windowing in Offline (System A) vs.\ Streaming (System B) Regimes:} In \textbf{System A (Offline Segmented Analysis)}, pre-segmented gesture instances are linearly resampled to a canonical length of $T=60$ frames ($\mathbf{X}_{\text{mm}} \in \mathbb{R}^{60 \times 21 \times 3}$) for standardized cross-benchmark comparison. Because whole-sequence resampling requires knowing gesture boundaries in advance and cannot be performed online, \textbf{System B (Causal Sliding-Window Streaming)} operates directly on a fixed-length first-in-first-out (FIFO) sliding window of the latest $W=60$ raw sensor frames at the native acquisition rate without temporal resampling.

\subsection{Visibility-Guarded Wrist-Centered $L_2$ Normalization: Offline vs.\ Causal Regimes}
Let $\mathbf{p}_{t,j} \in \mathbb{R}^3$ denote the 3D position (in mm) of joint $j$ at frame $t$, and let $v_{t,j} \in \{0,1\}$ denote its binary visibility indicator. When explicit per-joint confidence is not provided by the sensor SDK, we infer $\hat{v}_{t,j}$ causally from frame-to-frame kinematics: $\hat{v}_{t,j} = 0$ if $\|\mathbf{p}_{t,j}\|_2 < 10^{-3}\text{ mm}$ (zero-collapse), $\|\mathbf{p}_{t,j} - \mathbf{p}_{t-1,j}\|_2 < 10^{-5}\text{ mm}$ for $\ge 3$ consecutive frames (SDK buffer lock/freeze), or $\|\mathbf{p}_{t,j} - \mathbf{p}_{t-1,j}\|_2 > 60\text{ mm/frame}$ (unphysical jump). Notably, a naturally still human hand exhibits physiological micro-tremor and sensor thermal noise of $0.15\text{--}0.45\text{ mm/frame}$, whereas an SDK tracking freeze produces an exact zero bit-delta ($<10^{-5}\text{ mm}$), preventing false freeze triggers during static hand postures.

To prevent division-by-zero explosions and coordinate shifts when the wrist ($0$) or middle MCP joint ($9$) drops out, we first define the causal last-observed valid wrist anchor $\mathbf{p}_{t,0}^{\text{valid}}$ (with $t^* = \min\{k \mid v_{k,0}=1\}$) recursively as:
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{p}_{t,0}^{\text{valid}} = 
\begin{cases} 
\mathbf{p}_{t,0}, & \text{if } v_{t,0} = 1, \\
\mathbf{p}_{t-1,0}^{\text{valid}}, & \text{if } v_{t,0} = 0 \text{ and } t > 1, \\
\mathbf{p}_{t^*,0}, & \text{otherwise (or } \mathbf{0} \text{ if } v_{k,0}=0\ \forall k\text{)}.
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
Using $\mathbf{p}_{t,0}^{\text{valid}}$, we define the set of valid palm-reference frames as $\mathcal{T}_{\text{valid}} = \{t \in [1,T] \mid v_{t,0}=1 \wedge v_{t,9}=1 \wedge \|\mathbf{p}_{t,9} - \mathbf{p}_{t,0}^{\text{valid}}\|_2 > \tau_{\min}\}$ with $\tau_{\min} = 15.0\text{ mm}$ (adult human palm length is $70\text{--}100\text{ mm}$). We then define two distinct normalization operators:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{System A (Offline Non-Causal Normalization):} Uses the full sequence median $d_{\text{ref}}^{\text{off}} = \text{median}_{k \in \mathcal{T}_{\text{valid}}} \|\mathbf{p}_{k,9} - \mathbf{p}_{k,0}^{\text{valid}}\|_2$.
    \item \textbf{System B (Causal Streaming Normalization):} Uses only past and current valid frames $\mathcal{T}_{\text{valid}}^{\le t} = \{k \le t \mid k \in \mathcal{T}_{\text{valid}}\}$ to compute the running median $d_{\text{ref}}^{\text{causal}}(t) = \text{median}_{k \in \mathcal{T}_{\text{valid}}^{\le t}} \|\mathbf{p}_{k,9} - \mathbf{p}_{k,0}^{\text{valid}}\|_2$ with zero future-frame leakage.
\end{itemize}
In both systems, the normalized coordinate $\tilde{\mathbf{p}}_{t,j}$ is computed strictly relative to the valid wrist anchor $\mathbf{p}_{t,0}^{\text{valid}}$:
\begin{equation}
\tilde{\mathbf{p}}_{t,j} = v_{t,j} \cdot \frac{\mathbf{p}_{t,j} - \mathbf{p}_{t,0}^{\text{valid}}}{d_{t,\text{palm}}}, \quad d_{t,\text{palm}} = 
\begin{cases} 
\|\mathbf{p}_{t,9} - \mathbf{p}_{t,0}^{\text{valid}}\|_2, & t \in \mathcal{T}_{\text{valid}}, \\
d_{\text{ref}}, & t \notin \mathcal{T}_{\text{valid}},
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
defaulting to $d_{\text{default}} = 80.0\text{ mm}$ prior to the first valid frame (sensitivity tests across $d_{\text{default}} \in \{70, 80, 90, 100\}\text{ mm}$ confirm $<0.04\text{ mm}$ variance in $\text{MPJPE}_{\text{all}}$). This formulation guarantees deterministic handling across all four failure cases: (i) if wrist $0$ drops out, $\mathbf{p}_{t,0}^{\text{valid}}$ holds the last valid wrist position and $d_{t,\text{palm}} \leftarrow d_{\text{ref}}$; (ii) if middle MCP $9$ drops out or collapses ($\le 15\text{ mm}$), $t \notin \mathcal{T}_{\text{valid}}$ triggers $d_{t,\text{palm}} \leftarrow d_{\text{ref}}$; (iii) if both drop out, both guards activate simultaneously; and (iv) if initial frames $t=1\dots k$ are missing, $d_{t,\text{palm}} \leftarrow d_{\text{default}}$ prevents startup singularity.

\subsection{KAR-STGCN Architecture: System A (Offline) vs.\ System B (Causal)}
Given normalized coordinates $\tilde{\mathbf{X}} \in \mathbb{R}^{T \times 21 \times 3}$ and visibility mask $\mathbf{V} \in \{0,1\}^{T \times 21}$, we apply a two-stage dilated depthwise temporal bridge $\mathcal{B}_{\text{temp}}$ consisting of two cascaded 1D convolutions of kernel size $K_b=9$ with dilation factors $d_1=1$ and $d_2=2$, yielding an exact temporal receptive field of $\text{RF} = 1 + (K_b-1)d_1 + (K_b-1)d_2 = 1 + 8(1) + 8(2) = 25\text{ frames}$:
\begin{itemize}
    \item In \textbf{System A (Offline Restoration)}, symmetric padding yields a bidirectional receptive field of $25$ frames ($\pm 12$ frames lookahead buffer, corresponding to $100.0\text{ ms}$ at $120\text{ Hz}$ for LMC2 streams or $400.0\text{ ms}$ at $30\text{ Hz}$ for depth cameras).
    \item In \textbf{System B (Causal Sliding-Window Streaming)}, left-only causal padding restricts convolutions strictly to $\{t-24, \dots, t\}$, achieving a $25$-frame causal history with \textbf{zero lookahead delay ($0$ future frames / $0.0\text{ ms}$ lookahead buffer across all sensor frame rates)}.
\end{itemize}
The bridge imputes occluded coordinates via visibility gating:
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{X}_{\text{imp}} = \mathbf{V} \odot \tilde{\mathbf{X}} + (\mathbf{1} - \mathbf{V}) \odot \mathcal{B}_{\text{temp}}(\tilde{\mathbf{X}}).
\end{equation}
The concatenated tensor $\mathbf{F}_{\text{in}} = [\mathbf{X}_{\text{imp}} \,\|\, \mathbf{V}] \in \mathbb{R}^{T \times 21 \times 4}$ is projected to $C=64$ channels and processed through normalized skeletal graph convolutions $\hat{\mathbf{A}} = \mathbf{D}^{-1/2}(\mathbf{A}+\mathbf{I})\mathbf{D}^{-1/2}$ \cite{yan2018spatial} and multi-scale temporal convolutions ($3\times 1$ and $5\times 1$). The restored pose $\hat{\mathbf{X}}$ is obtained via a visibility-weighted residual gate ($\alpha_{\text{vis}} = 0.15$):
\begin{equation}
\hat{\mathbf{X}} = \mathbf{X}_{\text{imp}} + \left(\alpha_{\text{vis}}\mathbf{V} + (1-\alpha_{\text{vis}})(\mathbf{1}-\mathbf{V})\right) \odot \phi_{\text{dec}}(\mathbf{H}^{(L)}).
\end{equation}
For gesture classification, restored positions $\hat{\mathbf{X}}$ and first-order temporal velocities $\Delta\hat{\mathbf{X}}_t = \hat{\mathbf{X}}_t - \hat{\mathbf{X}}_{t-1}$ feed a batch-normalized dilated 1D convolutional head. To guarantee strict end-to-end causality in System B, first-order velocities are initialized causally at window boundaries ($\Delta\hat{\mathbf{X}}_1 = \mathbf{0}$), batch normalization layers freeze population running statistics during streaming inference, and coordinate denormalization uses $d_{\text{ref}}^{\text{causal}}(t)$ and $\mathbf{p}_{t,0}^{\text{valid}}$ (verified via bit-exact causal invariance unit tests in \texttt{causality\_test.py}). The joint training objective combines Smooth-$L_1$ coordinate loss, Bone-Length Consistency Error ($\mathcal{L}_{\text{bone}}$), and class-balanced cross-entropy ($\mathcal{L}_{\text{cls}}$):
\begin{align}
\mathcal{L}_{\text{bone}} &= \frac{1}{T|\mathcal{E}|} \sum_{t=1}^{T} \sum_{(u,v)\in\mathcal{E}} \left| \|\hat{\mathbf{p}}_{t,u} - \hat{\mathbf{p}}_{t,v}\|_2 - \|\mathbf{p}^*_{t,u} - \mathbf{p}^*_{t,v}\|_2 \right|, \\
\mathcal{L}_{\text{total}} &= \mathcal{L}_{\text{SmoothL1}}(\hat{\mathbf{X}}, \mathbf{X}^*) + \lambda_{\text{bone}}\mathcal{L}_{\text{bone}} + \lambda_{\text{cls}}\mathcal{L}_{\text{cls}}(\hat{\mathbf{y}}, y),
\end{align}
where $\lambda_{\text{bone}} = 0.60$, $\lambda_{\text{cls}} = 0.50$, and $\mathbf{p}^*_{t,u}, \mathbf{p}^*_{t,v}$ denote ground-truth joint positions in the normalized wrist-centered frame used exclusively during supervised training (never accessed during test-time inference).

\section{Experimental Protocol}

\subsection{Dataset Accounting and Subject-Independent Splits}
All evaluations strictly enforce \textbf{5-Fold Subject-Independent Cross-Validation} across four benchmarks categorized by sensing modality, ensuring zero subject overlap between training, validation, and testing partitions ($\mathcal{S}_{\text{train}} \cap \mathcal{S}_{\text{val}} \cap \mathcal{S}_{\text{test}} = \emptyset$; exact per-fold subject IDs are logged in \texttt{subject\_split\_manifest.csv}):
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{SHREC'17 \cite{desmedt2017shrec}} (\textit{Depth-Based Skeletal Benchmark}; $30\text{ fps}$): All $2,800$ raw sequences ($100\%$ retention) across $N=28$ independent subjects ($df=27$; 14-class and 28-class protocols; 3 folds of 6 test subjects and 2 folds of 5 test subjects, averaging $2,240.0$ train/val and $560.0$ test sequences per fold).
    \item \textbf{FPHA \cite{garcia2018first}} (\textit{Cross-Modal 6-DoF Magnetic Benchmark with Synthetic Missingness}; $30\text{ fps}$): All $1,175$ raw sequences ($100\%$ retention) across $N=6$ subjects and 45 action classes ($df=5$; grouped subject-independent partitioning with 4 folds holding out 1 test subject and 1 fold holding out 2 test subjects, averaging $940.0$ train/val and $235.0$ test sequences per fold).
    \item \textbf{LMDHG \cite{boulahia2017dynamic}} (\textit{First-Generation Optical LMC Benchmark}; $60\text{ fps}$): $608$ segmented dynamic gesture instances extracted from $50$ continuous recordings across $N=21$ subjects ($df=20$) and 13 classes (4 folds of 4 test subjects and 1 fold of 5 test subjects, averaging $486.4$ train/val and $121.6$ test instances per fold).
    \item \textbf{Orthogonal Dual-LMC2 (ML2HP) \cite{gilmartin2024dual}} (\textit{Cross-View Optical LMC2 Hardware Benchmark}; $120\text{ Hz}$): The raw repository contains $714,000$ multi-view sensor frame observations ($21\text{ subjects} \times 17\text{ postures} \times 2,000\text{ frames}$, forming $357,000$ time-synchronized stereo frame pairs). Following deterministic data selection ($\text{Raw } 714,000\text{ obs.} \rightarrow 357,000\text{ synced pairs} \rightarrow 2\text{ non-overlapping } 60\text{-frame windows per subject-posture trial}$), we obtain $21 \times 17 \times 2 = \mathbf{714\text{ evaluation windows}}$ ($42,840$ evaluated frames; $34$ windows/subject). Excluding $9$ windows ($1.26\%$) where both sensors dropped out simultaneously yields $705$ valid cross-view windows evaluated across 5 subject-independent folds (averaging $564.0$ train/val and $141.0$ test windows per fold).
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Ground-Truth and Reference Reliability}
We strictly distinguish three ground-truth evaluation regimes: (i) \textbf{Synthetic Held-Out Masking} on SHREC'17 and FPHA, where complete reference trajectories are corrupted by controlled dropouts; (ii) \textbf{Dual-Regime Evaluation} on LMDHG, separating Held-Out Contiguous Masking Restoration ($\text{MPJPE}_{\text{miss}}$ on verified high-confidence segments) from Natural Unmasked Recognition (Macro-F1 on raw sensor streams); and (iii) \textbf{Independent Secondary-Sensor Cross-View Reference} on Dual-LMC2, where Primary Sensor 1 undergoes natural unmasked self-occlusion and oblique dropouts ($18.4\%$ natural frame dropout rate), while Orthogonal Sensor 2 provides the reference trajectory. Sensor 2 is registered into Sensor 1's frame via Kabsch $SE(3)$ alignment over the first $30$ dropout-free calibration frames per subject, reducing pre-alignment offset from $48.20\text{ mm}$ to a registration residual of $\mathbf{1.84 \pm 0.42\text{ mm}}$ (median $p_{50}=1.76\text{ mm}, p_{95}=2.58\text{ mm}, \max=3.12\text{ mm}$; $1.62 \pm 0.35\text{ mm}$ in central vs.\ $2.38 \pm 0.49\text{ mm}$ in boundary workspace zones; sensitivity across $10, 20, 30, 50$ calibration frames shows convergence at $\ge 20$ frames with $<0.08\text{ mm}$ drift). Restoration error is evaluated strictly on frames where Sensor 1 drops out while Sensor 2 maintains valid tracking.

\subsection{Optical Dropout Simulation}
Calibrated against empirical LMDHG/Dual-LMC2 dropout distributions (burst lengths spanning $4\text{--}22$ frames, median $11$ frames), our simulator incorporates six failure modes: \textbf{Type A} (distal fingertip occlusion, $j \in \{4,8,12,16,20\}$), \textbf{Type B} (partial finger phalanx loss), \textbf{Type C} (instantaneous whole-hand loss), \textbf{Type D} (contiguous whole-hand burst dropout of $5\text{--}40$ frames), \textbf{Type E} (frozen-frame coordinate lock), and \textbf{Type F} (compound failure), grouped into four levels: \textbf{Level 1} (Jitter, $\sigma=0.8\text{ mm}$), \textbf{Level 2} (Type A+B Part Occlusion, $\sigma=2.2\text{ mm} + 20\%$ distal drop), \textbf{Level 3} (Type C+D+E Whole-Hand Burst, $\sigma=3.2\text{ mm} + 5\text{--}13$ frames complete 21-joint loss), and \textbf{Level 4} (Type F Compound Burst + 15\% Part Drop + 10:1 Imbalance, $\approx 26.5\%$ overall missing data).

\subsection{Training, Validation, and Reproduction Protocol}
To ensure strict baseline fairness, all competing imputers and GCNs receive an identical 20-trial hyperparameter search budget on validation subjects across learning rates $\{5\times 10^{-4}, 1\times 10^{-3}, 2.5\times 10^{-3}, 5\times 10^{-3}\}$ and weight decays $\{10^{-5}, 10^{-4}\}$ using Cosine Annealing and identical corrupted inputs, visibility masks $\mathbf{V}$, and subject splits (selecting optimal $\text{lr}=1\times 10^{-3}$ for SAITS \cite{du2023saits} and $2.5\times 10^{-3}$ for GCNs over 40 epochs, batch size 16, across 5 seeds: 42, 100, 2026, 777, 999). Complete configuration files (\texttt{experiment\_config.yaml}) and our \textbf{7-Stage Scientific Evidence Validation Pipeline}---comprising (1) data/split manifests (\texttt{dataset\_manifest.csv}, \texttt{subject\_split\_manifest.csv}), (2) Dual-LMC2 reference calibration logs (\texttt{reference\_quality\_report.csv}), (3) System B causality unit tests (\texttt{causality\_test.py}), (4) per-subject seed logs and automated table synchronization (\texttt{seed\_results.csv}, \texttt{verify\_tables.py}), (5) subject-level statistical notebooks (\texttt{statistical\_validation.ipynb}), (6) failure boundary matrices (\texttt{robustness\_matrix.csv}), and (7) 30-minute continuous $120\text{ Hz}$ deployment logs (\texttt{deployment\_benchmark.csv}, confirming $0\%$ frame drops and $245\text{ MB}$ VRAM over $216,000$ frames)---are archived in our public repository (Release Tag \texttt{v2.0.0}).

\subsection{Evaluation Metrics and Statistical Analysis}
We evaluate: (1) $\text{MPJPE}_{\text{all}}$ and occluded-joint $\text{MPJPE}_{\text{miss}}$ (mm), (2) Procrustes-aligned PA-MPJPE (mm), (3) Bone-Length Consistency Error (BLCE, mm), (4) Temporal Jitter ($\text{mm/frame}^2$, second-order acceleration error), (5) mean Joint Angle Error (JAE, degrees) and biomechanical Joint-Angle Violation Rate (JAVR $\%$, frames where any interphalangeal joint exceeds hyperextension $<-15^\circ$ or abduction $>25^\circ$), and (6) Macro-F1 ($\%$). To prevent seed-level pseudoreplication, multi-seed runs are first averaged within each independent subject prior to conducting omnibus Welch's ANOVA $F_W(8, df_2)$ across $K=9$ methods and two-sided paired $t$-tests with Holm-Bonferroni correction across the pre-specified family of $m=7$ primary benchmark comparisons in Table \ref{tab:statistical_significance}. On FPHA ($N=6, df=5$), subject-level paired tests are supplemented by a hierarchical LMM ($y_{ijk} = \beta_0 + \beta_1\text{Method}_i + u_{\text{subj}(j)} + v_{\text{seed}(k)} + \epsilon_{ijk}$ with Satterthwaite $df=23$).

