\section{Experimental Results}

\subsection{3D Hand Skeletal Restoration Performance}
Figure \ref{fig:mpjpe_bars} and Table \ref{tab:restoration_main} present the subject-independent restoration results across SHREC'17 ($N=28$, $2,800$ sequences) and FPHA ($N=6$, $1,175$ sequences). While VR filters ($1\text{\euro}$ Filter \cite{casiez20121euro}) and RTS Kalman smoothing perform adequately under Level 1 jitter ($\text{MPJPE}_{\text{all}} = 3.84\text{--}4.12\text{ mm}$), they fail under Level 3 whole-hand bursts ($\text{MPJPE}_{\text{miss}} = 42.18\text{ mm}$ and $32.45\text{ mm}$) and Level 4 compound dropouts ($\text{MPJPE}_{\text{miss}} = 48.90\text{ mm}$ and $37.82\text{ mm}$). Standard GCNs (ST-GCN, CTR-GCN, BlockGCN \cite{zhou2024blockgcn}) incur $\text{MPJPE}_{\text{miss}}$ of $17.64\text{--}24.12\text{ mm}$ on SHREC'17 Level 3 due to zero-filled feature propagation. Equipping CTR-GCN with SAITS imputation (\textbf{SAITS + CTR-GCN}) or our dilated bridge (\textbf{CTR-GCN + $\mathcal{B}_{\text{temp}}$}) reduces $\text{MPJPE}_{\text{miss}}$ from $19.08\text{ mm}$ to $14.12\text{ mm}$ and $12.95\text{ mm}$, confirming that explicit temporal bridging benefits standard GCNs. \textbf{System A (Offline KAR-STGCN)} achieves the lowest error across all regimes: $\text{MPJPE}_{\text{miss}} = \mathbf{9.42 \pm 0.44\text{ mm}}$ ($\text{MPJPE}_{\text{all}} = 4.18 \pm 0.21\text{ mm}$, $\text{BLCE} = 2.18 \pm 0.11\text{ mm}$) on SHREC'17 Level 3 (\textbf{50.6\%} reduction vs.\ CTR-GCN at $19.08\text{ mm}$), $\text{MPJPE}_{\text{miss}} = \mathbf{11.68 \pm 0.54\text{ mm}}$ ($\text{MPJPE}_{\text{all}} = 5.42 \pm 0.27\text{ mm}$, $\text{BLCE} = 2.74 \pm 0.14\text{ mm}$) on SHREC'17 Level 4 (\textbf{50.5\%} reduction vs.\ CTR-GCN at $23.58\text{ mm}$), and $\text{MPJPE}_{\text{miss}} = \mathbf{15.84 \pm 0.68\text{ mm}}$ ($\text{MPJPE}_{\text{all}} = 8.92 \pm 0.41\text{ mm}$, $\text{BLCE} = 3.85 \pm 0.19\text{ mm}$) on FPHA Level 4 (\textbf{45.8\%} reduction vs.\ CTR-GCN at $29.24\text{ mm}$). Crucially, \textbf{System B (Causal Sliding-Window KAR-STGCN)} achieves $\text{MPJPE}_{\text{miss}} = 10.28 \pm 0.48\text{ mm}$ on SHREC'17 Level 3 and $12.54 \pm 0.58\text{ mm}$ on Level 4 with \textbf{zero lookahead delay ($0.93\text{ ms}$ algorithmic streaming latency; $0.48\text{ ms}$ GPU forward pass)}, outperforming the two-stage offline SAITS + CTR-GCN baseline ($14.12\text{ mm}$ and $16.85\text{ mm}$ at $251.36\text{ ms}$ latency). Furthermore, benchmarking against dedicated motion completion architectures on SHREC'17 Level 4 confirms that a Spatial-Temporal Graph U-Net infiller achieves $\text{MPJPE}_{\text{miss}} = 13.84 \pm 0.64\text{ mm}$ ($\text{MPJPE}_{\text{all}} = 6.32\text{ mm}$, $\text{BLCE} = 3.38\text{ mm}$, $1.12\text{ ms}$ GPU forward pass) due to spatial pooling smoothing across distal phalanges, while a 50-step generative diffusion baseline \cite{rastgoo2025deep} achieves competitive offline restoration ($\text{MPJPE}_{\text{miss}} = 11.95 \pm 0.56\text{ mm}$, $\text{BLCE} = 2.82\text{ mm}$) but incurs $38.40\text{ ms}$ GPU forward latency ($138.85\text{ ms}$ total latency), exceeding the $8.33\text{ ms}$ real-time 120 Hz frame budget by $4.6\times$ whereas System B achieves $12.54\text{ mm}$ in $0.93\text{ ms}$ ($41\times$ faster GPU execution).

\subsection{Gesture Recognition and Real Leap Motion (LMDHG) Transfer}
Table \ref{tab:classification_main} reports subject-independent gesture recognition across SHREC'17 (14 and 28 classes) and FPHA (45 classes). Under Level 4 compound dropouts, System A and System B achieve \textbf{92.64\%} and \textbf{91.82\%} on SHREC'17-14, \textbf{86.42\%} and \textbf{85.54\%} on SHREC'17-28, and \textbf{82.35\%} and \textbf{81.40\%} on FPHA-45, outperforming DD-Net \cite{yang2019make}, BlockGCN \cite{zhou2024blockgcn}, and the two-stage SAITS + CTR-GCN pipeline.

Table \ref{tab:sim_to_real_lmdhg} evaluates transfer onto real first-generation Leap Motion streams from LMDHG \cite{boulahia2017dynamic} ($N=21$ subjects, $608$ gesture instances across 13 classes). Pre-training on our six-type dropout simulator (Protocol 2: Zero-Shot depth-to-LMC transfer without target fine-tuning) yields a held-out $\text{MPJPE}_{\text{miss}}$ of $12.74 \pm 0.58\text{ mm}$ and $82.45\%$ natural unmasked Macro-F1, while fine-tuning on LMDHG training subjects (Protocol 3) achieves $\text{MPJPE}_{\text{miss}} = \mathbf{9.86 \pm 0.45\text{ mm}}$ ($\text{MPJPE}_{\text{all}} = 4.35 \pm 0.21\text{ mm}$, $\text{BLCE} = 2.28 \pm 0.12\text{ mm}$) and $\mathbf{89.12 \pm 1.15\%}$ natural stream Macro-F1, reducing occluded-joint error by \textbf{44.0\%} compared to fine-tuned CTR-GCN ($17.62 \pm 0.82\text{ mm}$).

