\subsection{Natural Dropout Restoration on Orthogonal Dual-LMC2 (ML2HP) Hardware}
To evaluate natural optical dropouts against an independent secondary-sensor reference, Table \ref{tab:dual_lmc2} evaluates our framework on the \textbf{Orthogonal Dual-LMC2 (ML2HP) Benchmark} \cite{gilmartin2024dual} ($N=21$ subjects, 17 classes, $714$ evaluation windows). Primary Sensor 1 experiences natural self-occlusion and oblique whole-hand dropouts ($18.4\%$ missing rate), while Orthogonal Sensor 2 (registered via Kabsch $SE(3)$ alignment with $1.84 \pm 0.42\text{ mm}$ residual) provides the secondary-sensor reference trajectory. Zero-shot LMC1-to-LMC2 cross-sensor transfer (trained on LMDHG without Dual-LMC2 fine-tuning) already achieves $\text{MPJPE}_{\text{miss}} = 12.15 \pm 0.56\text{ mm}$ ($86.40\%$ F1), while in-domain \textbf{System B (Causal KAR-STGCN)} reduces cross-view $\text{MPJPE}_{\text{miss}}$ to $9.92 \pm 0.46\text{ mm}$ ($\text{MPJPE}_{\text{all}} = 3.98 \pm 0.19\text{ mm}$, $\text{BLCE} = 2.12\text{ mm}$, $\text{JAVR} = 1.62\%$, $93.10\%$ Macro-F1 at $0.93\text{ ms}$ latency), and \textbf{System A (Offline KAR-STGCN)} achieves $\text{MPJPE}_{\text{miss}} = \mathbf{9.18 \pm 0.42\text{ mm}}$ ($\text{MPJPE}_{\text{all}} = 3.68 \pm 0.18\text{ mm}$, $\text{BLCE} = 2.04 \pm 0.10\text{ mm}$, $\text{JAVR} = \mathbf{1.08\%}$, and $\mathbf{93.85 \pm 0.72\%}$ Macro-F1), outperforming BlockGCN \cite{zhou2024blockgcn} ($15.42\text{ mm}$) and CTR-GCN ($16.78\text{ mm}$) ($N=21$, $\Delta\text{MPJPE}_{\text{miss}} = -7.60 \pm 1.85\text{ mm}$, 95\% CI $[-8.44, -6.76]\text{ mm}$, $t(20) = -18.82$, Cohen's $d = 4.11$, $p_{\text{Holm}} < 0.0001$).

\section{Ablation and Robustness Analysis}

\subsection{Normalization Generalization and Reference-Bone Failure Robustness}
Table \ref{tab:norm_ablation} evaluates eight normalization strategies under SHREC'17 Level 4 across five explicit test-time hand scale factors ($\times 0.70, 0.85, 1.00, 1.15, 1.40$). All rows in Table \ref{tab:norm_ablation} and Table \ref{tab:ablation_burst_combined} use the exact same SHREC'17 Level 4 protocol as Table \ref{tab:restoration_main} (System A: $\text{MPJPE}_{\text{all}} = 5.42\text{ mm}$, $\text{BLCE} = 2.74\text{ mm}$, In-Scale Macro-F1 $= 92.64\%$). While raw coordinates with scale augmentation (Row 4) improve over unaugmented wrist-centering ($79.15\%$ vs.\ $51.20\%$ mean cross-scale F1), they still suffer an $8.10\%$ accuracy drop at extreme scales ($\times 0.70, \times 1.40$) and higher error ($\text{MPJPE}_{\text{all}} = 7.95\text{ mm}$). Furthermore, under severe reference-bone failures---including $\pm 15\text{ mm}$ wrist/middle-MCP drift and simultaneous wrist-plus-MCP dropout across $30\%$ of frames---naive instantaneous $L_2$ explodes ($>10^7\text{ mm}$), whereas \textbf{System B (Causal Running-Median)} and \textbf{System A (Offline Sequence-Median)} bound $\text{MPJPE}_{\text{all}}$ to $6.04\text{ mm}$ and $5.58\text{ mm}$ while maintaining consistent Macro-F1 across all five scales ($91.50\%\text{--}92.64\%$).

