}
\end{table*}

\subsection{Visibility Detector Validation, Mask-Label Corruption, and Burst Sweep}
We strictly distinguish \textbf{pose corruption} (physical coordinate loss under Level 4) from \textbf{mask-label corruption} (errors in visibility indicator $\mathbf{V}$). Evaluating our kinematic visibility detector $\hat{\mathbf{V}}$ directly against ground-truth occlusion masks across SHREC'17 Level 4 and Dual-LMC2 yields \textbf{Precision $= 96.4\%$, Recall $= 95.8\%$, and F1 $= 96.1\%$} (false positive rate $\text{FPR} = 1.2\%$, false negative rate $\text{FNR} = 4.2\%$, maintaining $95.9\%$ F1 during static hand pauses and $96.3\%$ during rapid motion). Table \ref{tab:ablation_burst_combined} (Left) compares the \textbf{Oracle Visibility Mask ($0\%$ mask-label error)}, \textbf{Inferred Kinematic Mask ($\hat{\mathbf{V}}$)}, \textbf{No Mask ($\mathbf{V}=\mathbf{1}$)}, and $5\%\text{--}20\%$ synthetic mask-label flip corruption. Replacing our kinematic-aware anatomical graph topology ($\hat{\mathbf{A}}$) with an unconstrained end-to-end learned adjacency matrix ($\mathbf{A}_{\text{learn}} \in \mathbb{R}^{21\times 21}$) degrades $\text{MPJPE}_{\text{all}}$ from $5.42\text{ mm}$ to $6.48 \pm 0.32\text{ mm}$ ($\text{MPJPE}_{\text{miss}} = 14.25 \pm 0.66\text{ mm}$, $\text{BLCE} = 3.64\text{ mm}$, Macro-F1 $= 89.40\%$), as unconstrained attention weights overfit to spurious cross-finger correlations when multiple digits drop out simultaneously. Decomposing $10\%$ mask-label error shows that \textbf{false negatives} ($\hat{v}_{t,j}=0$) incur minimal error ($\text{MPJPE}_{\text{all}} = 5.98\text{ mm}$, $91.20\%$ F1) because $\mathcal{B}_{\text{temp}}$ smoothly interpolates the joint, whereas $10\%$ \textbf{false positives} ($\hat{v}_{t,j}=1$) yield $6.68\text{ mm}$ ($89.75\%$ F1). Using $\hat{\mathbf{V}}$ without oracle $\mathbf{V}$ increases $\text{MPJPE}_{\text{all}}$ by only $0.26\text{ mm}$ ($5.68\text{ mm}$ vs.\ $5.42\text{ mm}$). Table \ref{tab:ablation_burst_combined} (Right) sweeps burst lengths across $5\text{--}40$ frames: within the $25$-frame receptive field, failure rates ($\text{MPJPE}_{\text{miss}} > 25\text{ mm}$) remain $\le 7.8\%$, rising sharply at $30\text{--}40$ frames ($33.80\text{ mm}$, $66.4\%$ failure).

