\subsection{Subject-Level Statistical Hypothesis Testing per Dataset}
To avoid seed-level pseudoreplication and avoid pooling heterogeneous datasets into a single composite test, Table \ref{tab:statistical_significance} reports statistical hypothesis tests \textbf{separately for each individual benchmark} across the family of $m=7$ comparisons: SHREC'17 ($N=28, df=27$), FPHA ($N=6, df=5$), LMDHG ($N=21, df=20$), and Dual-LMC2 ML2HP ($N=21, df=20$). On FPHA Level 4 ($N=6, df=5$), KAR-STGCN achieves a subject-level occluded-joint error reduction of $\Delta\text{MPJPE}_{\text{miss}} = -13.40 \pm 3.85\text{ mm}$ (95\% CI $[-17.44, -9.36]\text{ mm}$), yielding paired $t(5) = -8.53$ (raw $p = 0.00036$, Holm-adjusted $p_{\text{Holm}} = 0.0014$, exact two-sided Wilcoxon signed-rank $p = 0.03125$, and hierarchical LMM sensitivity analysis $F_{1,23} = 74.8, p < 10^{-6}$). Non-central $t$-distribution power analysis confirms that while $N=6$ ($df=5$) requires a minimum detectable effect size of $\text{MDES } d_z = 2.28$ at $80\%$ power ($\alpha_{\text{Holm}} = 0.00714$), our observed large effect sizes on FPHA ($d = 3.42\text{--}3.48$) achieve $>99.1\%$ statistical power. Notably, the large standardized effect sizes ($d = 2.95\text{--}4.11$, with subject-level paired difference $\text{SD}_{\Delta} = 1.85\text{--}3.85\text{ mm}$ and inter-quartile ranges logged in \texttt{seed\_results.csv}) reflect a structural physical mechanism rather than baseline under-tuning: feeding unmasked zero-dropped coordinates into standard GCNs corrupts local graph message passing across adjacent phalanges, whereas visibility-guarded bridging ($\mathcal{B}_{\text{temp}}$) deterministically eliminates zero-collapse propagation prior to graph convolution.

\section{Real-World Multimedia Application: KAR-Bench Studio}

\subsection{Anatomical Finger-Wise Analysis, 3D Animation Retargeting, and Three-Tier Latency}
To demonstrate direct utility for multimedia production and VR/AR systems, we developed \textbf{KAR-Bench Studio} (illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:studio_dashboard}), an open-source diagnostic and 3D character retargeting suite. KAR-Bench Studio integrates: (1) a live palm-bone oscilloscope monitoring $\|\mathbf{p}_{t,9} - \mathbf{p}_{t,0}\|_2$ stability, (2) a TorchScript deployment engine ($1.72\text{ MB}$ serialized model), (3) an automated Biovision Hierarchy (\texttt{.bvh}) skeletal exporter compatible with \textbf{Blender}, \textbf{Unity}, and \textbf{Unreal Engine}, and (4) a \textbf{Long-Burst Kinematic Fallback Guard} that smoothly damps joint velocities toward a neutral anatomical rest pose ($\hat{\mathbf{X}}_t \leftarrow \gamma_{\text{damp}}\hat{\mathbf{X}}_{t-1} + (1-\gamma_{\text{damp}})\mathbf{X}_{\text{rest}}$ with $\gamma_{\text{damp}}=0.92$) whenever contiguous tracking loss exceeds the $25$-frame receptive field, preventing mesh self-intersection during prolonged sensor dropouts.

We profile latency across three distinct tiers on an NVIDIA RTX GPU (with CUDA synchronization over $1,000$ streaming windows and a continuous 30-minute $120\text{ Hz}$ stress test over $216,000$ frames logged in \texttt{deployment\_benchmark.csv}): (i) \textbf{Model Latency} (GPU forward pass only): median $p_{50} = 0.48\text{ ms}$ ($p_{95} = 0.52\text{ ms}, p_{99} = 0.55\text{ ms}$); (ii) \textbf{Algorithmic Streaming Latency} (CPU visibility guard + running-median normalization + GPU forward pass + denormalization): $p_{50} = 0.93\text{ ms}$ ($p_{95} = 0.99\text{ ms}, p_{99} = 1.04\text{ ms}$); and (iii) \textbf{Application Retargeting Latency} (sensor frame ingestion + algorithmic restoration + Euler joint-angle conversion for \texttt{.bvh} rig retargeting): $p_{50} = 1.42\text{ ms}$ ($p_{95} = 1.54\text{ ms}, p_{99} = 1.65\text{ ms}$), remaining well below the $8.33\text{ ms}$ frame budget of $120\text{ Hz}$ optical hand trackers with $0$ dropped frames.

