Table \ref{tab:finger_breakdown} and Figure \ref{fig:studio_dashboard} decompose restoration errors across the five fingers specifically on SHREC'17 Level 4 and report BLCE, mean \textbf{Joint Angle Error (JAE in degrees)}, and downstream \textbf{Joint-Angle Violation Rate (JAVR $\%$)}. Because the ring and pinky fingers suffer the highest self-occlusion rates, the $1\text{\euro}$ Filter \cite{casiez20121euro} and RTS Kalman Smoother exhibit severe pinky errors ($25.12\text{ mm}$ and $19.28\text{ mm}$), high JAE ($21.40^\circ$ and $15.80^\circ$), and high JAVR ($31.40\%$ and $19.85\%$). Both \textbf{System B (Causal KAR-STGCN)} and \textbf{System A (Offline KAR-STGCN)} leverage cross-finger graph adjacency ($\hat{\mathbf{A}}$) and $\mathcal{L}_{\text{bone}}$ to constrain pinky error to $7.24\text{ mm}$ and $\mathbf{6.68\text{ mm}}$, reduce JAE to $4.58^\circ$ and $\mathbf{4.12^\circ}$, and reduce JAVR to $1.78\%$ and $\mathbf{1.15\%}$ on SHREC'17 Level 4 (and $1.62\%$ and $\mathbf{1.08\%}$ on Dual-LMC2 in Table \ref{tab:dual_lmc2}), preserving natural finger articulation without rigid-hand lock.

\section{Discussion, Limitations, and Failure Cases}
We categorize the limitations of our study into three areas:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Sensor-Related Limitations (Reference Noise Floor and Simultaneous Dropouts):} On the Orthogonal Dual-LMC2 (ML2HP) benchmark \cite{gilmartin2024dual} (Table \ref{tab:dual_lmc2}), restoration is evaluated against a Kabsch-aligned secondary LMC2 sensor ($1.84 \pm 0.42\text{ mm}$ registration residual). As established by Matuszczyk et al.\ \cite{matuszczyk2025comparative}, the secondary LMC2 sensor carries an intrinsic palm positional error of $5.2\text{--}5.3\text{ mm}$ in the central zone and higher variance near workspace boundaries, and $1.26\%$ of windows experience simultaneous dual-sensor dropouts. Thus, our cross-view $\text{MPJPE}_{\text{miss}}$ of $9.18\text{ mm}$ reflects secondary-sensor cross-view consistency rather than sub-millimeter studio ground truth.
    \item \textbf{Model-Related Limitations and Operational Boundary (``When Not to Use''):} As illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:qualitative_hand}(d) and quantified in Table \ref{tab:ablation_burst_combined} (Right), when a whole-hand dropout exceeds the $25$-frame receptive field of $\mathcal{B}_{\text{temp}}$ (e.g., $30\text{--}40$ frames, or $>250\text{ ms}$ at $120\text{ Hz}$), the temporal bridge loses boundary anchor frames and regresses toward a static, semi-flexed mean hand posture. Thus, KAR-STGCN is specifically designed for short-to-medium optical burst dropouts ($\le 25$ frames) under sub-millisecond streaming constraints, and \textit{should not be used} as a standalone generative motion synthesizer for long-horizon tracking loss ($>0.5\text{--}1.0\text{ s}$) where the hand completely exits the sensor workspace; in such regimes, KAR-Bench Studio activates its kinematic rest-pose damping guard, whereas offline animation pipelines may prefer slower multi-step diffusion motion priors \cite{rastgoo2025deep} ($>25\text{--}80\text{ ms}$ latency).
    \item \textbf{Application-Related Limitations (Sensor Frame Rates and End-to-End Display Latency):} SHREC'17 \cite{desmedt2017shrec} and FPHA \cite{garcia2018first} are captured at $\approx 30\text{ fps}$ ($12$-frame offline lookahead $= 400\text{ ms}$), whereas LMDHG \cite{boulahia2017dynamic} and Dual-LMC2 \cite{gilmartin2024dual} operate at up to $60\text{--}120\text{ Hz}$ ($100\text{ ms}$ lookahead in System A; $0\text{ frames}/0.0\text{ ms}$ lookahead in System B). Moreover, while our three-tier profiling measures Model ($0.48\text{ ms}$), Algorithmic ($0.93\text{ ms}$), and Application \texttt{.bvh} Retargeting ($1.42\text{ ms}$) latencies, complete deployed VR display photon latency additionally depends on external USB polling and rendering engine v-sync.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}
This paper introduced KAR-STGCN, a visibility-guarded $L_2$ palm-bone normalization scheme (formulated in both offline sequence-median and causal sliding-window running-median regimes), and the KAR-Bench Studio retargeting suite for 3D hand skeletal restoration and gesture recognition. By preventing division-by-zero singularities, mitigating scale shortcuts, and enforcing anatomical bone consistency across $25$-frame dilated neighborhoods, KAR-STGCN consistently outperforms VR filters, time-series imputers, and skeleton GCNs across four complementary benchmarks: SHREC'17 ($N=28$), FPHA ($N=6$), LMDHG ($N=21$), and Dual-LMC2 ML2HP ($N=21$).

